*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Because your combined interest ($2,650) and dividends ($670) exceed $1,500, the IRS requires you to list them on this form.
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** You will report your $18,400 photography income here, and deduct your $1,050 in supplies.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** You will use this form to calculate your $3,402 actual-expense home office deduction, which then carries over to your Schedule C.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** You must pay Medicare and Social Security taxes on your net photography income (Income minus supplies and home office). This form calculates that tax.
*   **Schedule 2 (Additional Taxes):** This is where your Self-Employment tax from Schedule SE gets attached to your main return.

**Summary of your taxable picture:**
Your net business income will be roughly **$13,948** ($18,400 - $1,050 supplies - $3,402 home office). You will pay self-employment tax on that $13,948, and it will be added to your $68,200 W-2 income and $3,320 investment income. Then, your $2,500 student loan deduction and $14,600 standard deduction will be subtracted to find your final taxable income.
